Kevin Rajan’s view is that “code is the great equalizer”: software—and increasingly LLMs—can distribute knowledge and engineering ability beyond traditional gatekeepers.

On humanitarian automation, he argues that civilization progresses when important actions become reliable and routine. His goal is therefore to automate systems around health, infrastructure, and open access so that:

- more people gain practical agency;
- competence compounds without gatekeepers;
- ideas can become working software more easily;
- essential work happens with less repeated human effort;
- abundance and quality of life improve broadly.

In short, he treats automation not merely as productivity tooling, but as infrastructure for democratizing capability. The public statement is aspirational rather than a detailed humanitarian policy framework. [Kevin Rajan’s canonical build log](https://kvnloo.github.io/boplog/#about).
